247	5. implement two-phase `restore(data, stored_version)` (decode and validate into scratch, swap on success) returning a value `(ok, reason, detail)` with `reason` from the block's own closed `<BLOCK>_RESTORE_*` set (the Graph's `GRAPH_RESTORE_*` is the model); a non-conforming return (null, wrong type, a reason outside the declared set) is `SL_RESTORE_NONCONFORMING` and is a hard failure — GDScript has no exceptions, so a runtime script error yields a garbage return [UNV];
248	6. treat out-of-range values as a hard failure, **never clamp** ("a corrupt save must not be fixed into a different world");
249	7. provide `default_state()` and, for a block added after format 1, `added_in_format`;
250	8. in `post_restore()` resolve cross-block IDs, rebuild caches, check invariants, and set `previous := current` for every moving read view (Pipeline Interactions row 1, PC-17) so the first frame after a load does not interpolate from a stale pose;
251	9. track dirty keys explicitly in its mutators — Godot 4.7 packed-array element writes do not call the property setter (sourced) — so any cached hash the block exposes stays honest;
252	10. be **restore-idempotent**: `snapshot(restore(snapshot()))` equals `snapshot()` in canonical bytes — the kernel's own rule (K:839: the round trip yields byte-identical canonical bytes for every registered block, whose byte-identity assertion K-3 retains while it changes only the transport; the Graph's instance is G:1096). A block whose restore normalises transient fields does not conform (the Controller resolved this by normalising in `snapshot()`, PC-17; `interaction.state` and `assembly.state` are the remaining cases, OQ-39, and the routed CONFLICT of OQ-9 is closed for the Controller and Body); until their owners move the clears into `snapshot()` the build constant `REBASE_BLOCKS` (SL-12) names such blocks, and it must be empty at the first outside build *(Amended 2026-10-27 — design review)*;
253	11. register its migration steps per version and a golden save per shipped version (SL-14);
254	12. key `PER_PLAYER` data by PlayerId inside its own dictionaries, and pass the generic round-trip test every block has (`snapshot → var_to_bytes → bytes_to_var → restore → snapshot` equal, plus a JSON-dump assertion that 64-bit values arrive as strings — K-3).
255	
256	Enforcement is by **boot assertions in the Save/Load service**, not by the kernel; a violation fails Save/Load's own boot — the composition root refuses to start, naming the block and the rule — never a runtime surprise. Whether that surfaces as the kernel's `BOOTING → FAULTED` is the composition root's mechanism (routed to the kernel owner; `FAULTED`'s triggers are kernel-owned, K:159):
257	
258	| Reason | Condition |
259	|---|---|
260	| `SL_BOOT_NO_SUPPORTED_VERSIONS` | a `persist` block declares no `supported_versions`, or the list lacks its own current `schema_version` |
261	| `SL_BOOT_RESTORE_AFTER_UNREGISTERED` | a `restore_after` target is not a registered block id |
262	| `SL_BOOT_PERCEPTION_PERSIST` | `class PERCEPTION_OUT` with `persist = true` (CR-12) |
263	| `SL_BOOT_SCOPE_MISSING` | `persist = true` without `persist_scope` (catches the Sanity row gap mechanically) |
264	| `SL_BOOT_PER_PLAYER_UNKEYED` | a `PER_PLAYER` block whose fixture snapshot does not key its data by PlayerId |
265	| `SL_BOOT_NO_ADDED_IN_FORMAT` | a persisted block has no `added_in_format` entry in the format data file |
266	| `SL_BOOT_RETIRED_BUT_REGISTERED` | an id is on `RETIRED_BLOCKS` and also registered |
267	| `SL_BOOT_STRINGNAME_KEY` / `SL_BOOT_TYPED_CONTAINER` | the fixture snapshot contains a `StringName` key or a typed container |
268	| `SL_BOOT_SAVELOAD_REGISTERS_BLOCK` | Save/Load itself registered a block or hook (SL-17) |
